e-August and September polls show the race tied to R+1, tighter than the Lean R expert consensus", "Salazar's large financial edge (about $2.3M to $325K cash on hand) in an R+6 district still favors her", "Prediction markets price Rodriguez around 38%, modestly better for Democrats than most expert ratings imply"], "rationale": "Salazar's incumbency, fundraising advantage and the district's Republican lean keep her favored, but the public Trump-Salazar feud combined with deportation politics has produced genuine crossover pressure, and the two most recent polls show the race tied or R+1. That supports a narrow Republican edge with above-average uncertainty.", "watch": "An escalation of the Trump feud, further erosion among Cuban-American voters over deportations, or a late Democratic spending surge could tip this near-tied race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Representative María Elvira Salazar, a Republican, seeks another term in this Miami-area district that leaned about six points Republican at the presidential level. She enters October with a large financial edge, roughly $2.3 million on hand against about $325,000 for Democrat Eliott Rodriguez. Most professional raters call the race Lean Republican, though RealClearPolitics rates it a Tossup and betting markets give Rodriguez roughly a 38 percent chance.\n\nThe race has been unsettled by an unusual dynamic: President Trump has attacked Salazar on social media even as Democrats tie her to his deportation policies, an issue resonating in Cuban-American Miami. Two September polls from Democratic-affiliated firms showed the contest tied or Salazar ahead by a point, and local reporting describes the race as tightening. Salazar's incumbency, money and the district's partisan lean still make her the favorite, but the margin appears narrow.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2129, "margin": -4.43}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2851,